insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('9f6293b9-780e-5162-bb44-564d2327e576', 'rg6-text-structure-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-text-structure'), null,
        '6-8', $q$How a Hot Air Balloon Rises$q$, $q$Early on a calm morning, a crew spreads a huge nylon balloon across a grassy field. In less than an hour, that flat pile of fabric will be floating high above the trees. Getting it there takes several careful steps, and the crew follows them in the same order every time.

First, the crew lays the balloon, called the envelope, on the ground and attaches it to a wicker basket. The basket is tipped on its side so the opening of the envelope faces a large fan.

Next, the fan blows cool air into the envelope. As the air rushes in, the fabric slowly puffs up like a giant sleeping bag being shaken out. Two crew members hold the opening wide so the air can flow freely.

After that, the pilot lights the burner, which sits just above the basket. A long flame shoots into the envelope and heats the air inside. Warm air is lighter than the cooler air around it, so the envelope begins to lift and stand upright. It pulls the basket upright too.

Finally, the pilot and passengers climb in. The pilot fires the burner again, and the balloon rises gently off the ground. To go higher, the pilot adds more heat. To come down, the pilot lets the air cool or opens a small vent at the top.

A balloon has no steering wheel, though. It drifts wherever the wind carries it. That is why a chase crew follows in a truck, ready to meet the balloon wherever it lands.$q$, 'draft')
