-- Passage rg6-text-structure-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('a73f466a-d766-5a82-95ae-06c31814f522', 'rg6-text-structure-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-text-structure'), null,
        '6-8', $q$From Wooden Runner to Modern Bike$q$, $q$The bicycle you ride today took about two hundred years to develop. Each new design fixed a problem with the one that came before it, and sometimes the fix created a brand new problem.

The earliest version, built in the early 1800s, had two wooden wheels and a seat but no pedals. Riders pushed along the ground with their feet, a little like riding a scooter. It was fun on smooth paths but tiring on hills.

In the 1860s, inventors attached pedals directly to the front wheel. Now riders could keep their feet off the ground. However, the wheels were made of wood and iron, and the ride was so bumpy that people nicknamed these machines "boneshakers."

Builders soon learned that a bigger front wheel traveled farther with each turn of the pedals. By the 1870s, some front wheels were taller than a grown man. These high-wheel bicycles were fast, but riders sat so high that a small rock could send them tumbling over the handlebars.

The answer came in the 1880s with the "safety bicycle." It had two wheels of the same size and a chain that connected the pedals to the back wheel. Riders sat low and could put a foot down to stop. Soon after, air-filled rubber tires made the ride much smoother.

Modern bikes still follow the safety bicycle's basic shape. Lighter metals, many gears, and better brakes have been added, but the main idea has lasted well over a hundred years.$q$, 'draft')
