insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('681d2208-e682-5f29-9ab6-c9d9439e5945', 'rg6-text-structure-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-text-structure'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Alligator or Crocodile?$q$, $q$At a glance, alligators and crocodiles look almost the same. Both are large reptiles with thick, scaly skin, long tails, and powerful jaws. Both spend much of their time in water and lay their eggs on land. Still, if you know where to look, you can tell them apart.

The easiest clue is the shape of the snout. An alligator has a wide, rounded snout shaped like the letter U. A crocodile's snout is narrower and more pointed, closer to the shape of a V.

The teeth offer another clue. When an alligator closes its mouth, its upper jaw covers most of its lower teeth, so you mainly see the top row. When a crocodile closes its mouth, a large tooth on each side of the lower jaw still sticks up. This gives the crocodile a toothy grin.

Color can help too. Alligators are usually dark gray or nearly black. Crocodiles tend to be lighter, often olive green or tan.

Finally, the two animals often live in different kinds of water. Alligators mostly stay in fresh water, such as swamps, rivers, and lakes. Many crocodiles have special glands that remove extra salt from their bodies, so they can also live in salty water along the coast.

Only one place in the world, the southern tip of Florida, is home to both animals in the wild. Visitors there who remember these clues can name each reptile from a safe distance.$q$, 'draft')
