-- Passage rg6-text-structure-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('f4162d7b-9cd0-5fb5-929c-820a1dc807c0', 'rg6-text-structure-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-text-structure'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Daily Life on a Space Station$q$, $q$Astronauts aboard a space station circle Earth about every ninety minutes. Because everything inside floats, ordinary tasks like eating, sleeping, and exercising work very differently than they do at home.

Mealtime is the first challenge. Food on the station comes in sealed pouches and cans. Crumbs are a real problem, because they could float into equipment or into someone's eyes. For this reason, astronauts eat tortillas instead of bread. Drinks are sipped through straws from closed bags so the liquid does not drift away in wobbly blobs.

Sleep brings its own puzzle. There is no up or down in space, so astronauts can sleep facing any direction. Each one has a tiny cabin about the size of a closet. Astronauts zip themselves into sleeping bags tied to the wall so they do not drift into anything during the night.

Exercise may be the most important task of all. Without gravity pulling on them, muscles and bones slowly grow weaker. To stay strong, astronauts exercise about two hours every day. They run on a treadmill while wearing straps that hold them down, and they use machines that work like lifting weights.

Life on the station takes careful planning, from every meal to every workout. Yet crew members often say that one sight makes up for all the trouble. Because the station moves so fast, they can watch about sixteen sunrises every single day.$q$, 'draft')
